+# Talk notes
+
+Target: 30:00. Short cues, spoken, not sentences. Timings are for pacing, not slides.
+
+## 0:00 Intro (1:30)
+
+>> advance: Ownership
+- Fel, infrastructure at LiveKit
+- Quick question: what do we actually run on, and who controls it?
+- Today most of the stack is proprietary
+- Firmware is a blob, boot path signed by someone else, image locked to one build, control plane rented
+- You can use the machine, you can't inspect or replace the parts that go into it
+- So: how much of it can you own, and how far does ownership go?
+- I'll argue today: you should be able to own everything, metal, firmware, kernel, image, trust, cluster
+- Owning it means I hold the keys, I can rebuild it, and I can prove what runs
+
+>> advance: The outline
+- Seven demos, six layers, blank board to cluster, one layer at a time
+- Silicon, enablement, firmware, image, trust, cluster
+- Every demo is a runbook or a recording, no mockups, nothing manual
+
+## 1:30 Silicon (2:30)
+
+>> advance: Decades of legacy
+- First layer is silicon, and the real question: why a new ISA at all?
+- x86 and ARM carry decades of legacy
+- Licenses are expensive, and the terms are private
+- x86: only two companies can make the chips
+- ARM: design your own core, get sued, as Qualcomm shows
+- x86: management engine and PSP below the OS, firmware is a pile of blobs
+- ARM: TrustZone in every core, a black box
+- No standard boot path on ARM, downstream kernels fork and drift, some just go away
+
+>> advance: RVA23
+- 2010, Berkeley, a clean ISA for research, small base, optional extensions
+- Base frozen, software does not break
+- An ISA is the contract between compiler and chip, the binary records it
+- Missing feature fails on the first instruction, not at link time
+- Profiles bundle extensions for a market, RVA23 is the current application profile
+- Vector and virtualization are mandatory, you name the profile once, no vendor in the name
+- Anything RVA23-compliant runs the same binary
+
+>> advance: The timeline so far
+- 2011 spec public, 2014 base frozen, 2015 foundation
+- 2017 first open SoC, 2021 vector and hypervisor ratified, D1 ships
+- 2023 RVA20 and RVA22, 2024 RVA23 and the K1
+- 2026 K3, Alibaba C950 at 3.2 GHz, SpacemiT X200 core done
+- RISC-V is about a quarter of global chip production now
+- Profile this talk uses landed two years ago, and the boards are here
+
+>> advance: The K3 processor
+- This is the K3, two kinds of core
+- 8 X100 run Linux and control plane, 8 A100 are RVA22 and share memory, you could run a separate Linux there
+- Vector 256 on X100, 1024 on A100, length-agnostic, one binary for both and QEMU
+- 60 TOPS INT4, 30B model at 15 tokens per second, no GPU needed
+
+>> advance: What comes next
+- K3 is the very beginning, first commercially available RVA23 chip
+- Alibaba C950, SiFive P550, SpacemiT X200 next, Tenstorrent Atlantis
+- RVA23 becomes the baseline, many implementations, same software
+- Chip alone is not a platform, next the software
+
+## 4:00 Software (1:15)
+
+>> advance: Software support
+- The software supported and sometimes mandated RVA23 before the chips were out
+- Ubuntu mandates RVA23 now
+- RHEL 10 has a RISC-V build
+- Debian 13, riscv64 official
+- Android 17 on RVA23 hardware via RISE, last month
+- Podman, Go, k3s all work
+- AMD GPUs plug into PCIe like on x86, PowerVR has a mainline driver
+- P550 by SiFive runs games via Felix86, an x86 emulation layer
+- This laptop runs GNOME OS, and ParticleOS is the systemd team's mkosi build
+- But you still need hands on the board
+
+## 5:15 Enablement (2:30)
+
+>> advance: Pushrod
+- Pushrod gives an agent remote hands on real hardware
+- Flash, boot, watch the screen, type, retry
+- Nobody presses a button or copies a log by hand
+- That is autonomous hardware enablement
+- I've done a lot of board bring-up, pressing volume and power gets old
+
+>> advance: The button presser
+- Blank board enters flash mode only by pressing two buttons, no software path
+- No probe soldered on the board, replacement is expensive or impossible
+- Two solenoids over RST and FDL, printed holder, cents
+- Doctorow's reverse centaur: human serving the machine, we flip it
+
+>> advance: The harness
+- USB relay board drives the fingers, 150 ms, capped at 5 s per 60 s
+- NanoKVM-USB: HDMI capture, keyboard and mouse
+- UART at 115200 for the console before the screen exists
+- Tigard JTAG, work in progress
+- Fastboot over USB, Titanflasher protocol reimplemented in Go
+
+>> advance: MCP server
+- One Go server turns the desk into typed tools, not shell strings
+- Stateless, files go over the wire, no shared disk
+- Remote over Tailscale, a cloud agent drives the board on your desk
+- Localhost only unless you publish
+
+>> advance: Tool naming
+- relay.* board control, target.* button sequences
+- serial.* console, files.* uploads
+- flash.* images and partitions, kvm.* screen and input
+
+>> advance: Flash order
+- Read the devices first, DFU is BROM, U-Boot is fastboot
+- BROM answers OKAY and writes nothing, stage FSBL, continue
+- Stage U-Boot, continue, read again
+- Build the tables from offset and size, 6 MTD and 12 GPT
+- One multiFlash writes everything, watch sentBytes and totalBytes
+
+## 7:45 Build (1:45)
+
+>> advance: Images are the distributable asset
+- Images are what we ship, one signature, one switch, one rollback
+
+>> advance: Buildroot tree
+- Buildroot 2001, make and Kconfig, no package manager, image is the unit
+- Hacky tree for this talk, production wants mkosi or BuildStream
+- One tree, two defconfigs, K3 and QEMU
+- ukify and sbsign, mkfs.erofs and veritysetup, systemd-repart, genimage
+- Outputs: ext4 root, two EROFS slots, verity and sig, ESP, three UKIs per slot, extensions
+
+>> advance: Disk layout
+- One unified layout for both boards, sysupdate fills the second usr slot
+- The K3 also uses systemd-boot now, same chain as QEMU
+
+## 9:30 Demo 1 (1:15)
+
+>> advance: Demo 1
+- Demo one is a recording, the flash takes about ten minutes end to end
+- Blank board, two solenoids, runbook
+- One prompt, the agent uploads, resets, stages, writes every partition
+- Progress to 100 percent, reset, EDK2, boot, shell
+- That shell came from a boot chain, so next the chain
+
+## 10:45 Firmware (1:15)
+
+>> advance: From a blank chip to a boot menu
+- K3: BROM, FSBL, ESOS, OpenSBI, EDK2, systemd-boot, UKI
+- QEMU: SPL, OpenSBI, EDK2, systemd-boot, UKI
+- Same image, same UKI layout, two boot paths
+- K3 leaves the chain unmeasured, no TPM, Secure Boot only with eFuses, we don't blow them
+- QEMU measures, swtpm, full chain
+
+>> advance: What we could prove
+- Half check on K3: Secure Boot if we blew the eFuses, we keep the board debuggable
+- Full check on QEMU: Secure Boot and a software TPM
+- Build checks both: modified UKI fails Secure Boot, modified system data fails verity
+
+## 12:00 Demo 2 (1:15)
+
+>> advance: Demo 2
+- Demo two is a recording, the board shows a console
+- The image ships Weston off, one file enables it
+- The agent writes the file, the desktop comes up
+- It refreshes the signed graphics extension, terminal and gears appear
+- Screenshots, terminal test, EGL gears, all from the extension
+- Fix arrived as a signed extension, so next the image model
+
+## 13:15 Image model (1:45)
+
+>> advance: A package manager rewrites usr in place
+- The package model: files in place, scripts as root, rollback is reinstall
+- The image model: the image is the unit, a switch is one boot entry
+
+>> advance: Two generations before this talk
+- CoreOS: A/B slots, bootloader flips
+- rpm-ostree: layering, old commit stays on disk
+- bootc: OCI image, own boot entry, registry as channel
+- composefs: EROFS plus fs-verity, digest in the UKI, Secure Boot checks it
+
+>> advance: What this talk uses instead
+- sysupdate, not bootc, because I want to extend the root, not the image
+- DDI: one disk image with a release file, verity
+- sysext adds a subtree of usr, confext adds a subtree of etc
+- bootc marks sysext unsupported, overlay depth, so there you extend the image
+- Both work, the choice is where the update lands
+
+>> advance: Who else ships this
+- GNOME OS, BuildStream, sysupdate
+- ParticleOS, mkosi and OBS, IPE
+- Amutable OS, image-based, sysext and confext, signed report ledger
+
+## 15:00 Demo 3 (1:15)
+
+>> advance: Demo 3
+- Demo three is a recording, registry on the laptop, tunnel to the board
+- updatectl verifies signed checksums, stages version 3 in the inactive slot
+- bootctl set-default, reboot, board reports three
+- Extensions merge, graphics, k3s, podman
+- Signed update, but a signed file does not prove what runs at boot
+
+## 16:15 Trust (2:30)
+
+>> advance: A TPM is a small crypto processor
+- TPM: keys and measurements
+- EK identifies the chip, AK signs quotes
+- Measurements land in PCRs
+
+>> advance: A PCR only extends
+- PCR is a hash register, new value is hash of old value and measurement
+- Never set back, so the history is in the value
+- Firmware measures each stage, UKI stub measures the UKI into PCR 11
+- PCR 11 answers one question: which UKI booted
+
+>> advance: Remote Attestation Challenges
+- Verifier sends a nonce, recorded quote cannot replay
+- TPM signs a quote over the PCRs with the AK
+- Verifier checks the signature against the enrolled AK
+- Consumes the nonce, compares the PCRs with the profile
+- The event log explains the values, one line only, the slide is gone
+
+>> advance: pcrlock and systemd-report
+- pcrlock predicts the PCRs from the log, local policy, TPM unlocks without a remote verifier
+- systemd-report exports the report, one signature per signer
+- Receiver keeps the receipt
+
+## 17:45 Demo 4 (1:00)
+
+>> advance: Demo 4
+- Demo four runs on QEMU, ten seconds, warm guest
+- Secure Boot with our keys, swtpm
+- The guest uploads a signed report
+- Receiver answers accepted, componentsVerified, eventLogRecords, and the mechanism reads TPM2
+- The view shows the same receipt
+- This is fleet management: every node sends a signed report about its own state
+- A node that drifts stands out
+
+## 18:45 Consent (1:45)
+
+>> advance: Attestable User Consent
+- We proved the machine, now a payment needs a person
+- Two things: key residency, provable consent
+- Key residency: payment secret stays in the TPM
+- Consent: a human approved this payment, and the approval is provable
+- Two ways: trusted host OS, attested, or a separate security key or smartcard
+- The second keeps end-user freedom, no OS attestation needed
+
+>> advance: Assurance Level Tiers
+- Tier 0: small spending, TPM pays, host consent, daily limit, no replay
+- Tier 1: development spending, host, FIDO2, or smartcard
+- Development mode is like ADB on Android, it trades assurance for convenience
+- Tier 2: production spending, OS report matches, or a key or card
+- The wallet keeps the quote and the consent signature as the receipt
+
+## 20:30 Demo 5 (1:00)
+
+>> advance: Demo 5
+- Demo five runs on QEMU, about eighty seconds
+- The guest proves its boot chain, then reads PCR 11 and restarts the wallet with that profile
+- FIDO2 is an emulated U2F device, the smartcard an emulated CCID card
+- Tier 0 host, tier 1 all three paths, tier 2 host and FIDO2
+- Nonce single use, every accepted path in the wallet view
+- The U2F registration attestation and the smartcard PKCS#11 signature are the extra signals
+- The same exchange can run the other way, client attests server, mutual attestation
+
+## 21:30 Fleet (2:00)
+
+>> advance: Secure Fleet Management Primitives
+- Now we have all four: signed image, signed update, attested boot, provable consent
+- A fleet can be very secure from day one, IoT, clients, Kubernetes nodes
+- sysexts: base usr plus signed sysext, merged with overlay, IPE gate
+- Only signed code executes at the kernel level
+
+>> advance: Demo 6
+- Demo six runs on the K3, one node, under a minute after warm-up
+- k3s from a signed extension, node Ready
+- Valkey pod, RESP over IPv6, PONG
+- Tamper one byte of the k3s extension, refresh rejects it
+- Restore, pod keeps running
+- I have more demos ready: QEMU + K3, RVA22 bare metal, RVA23 beta, hybrid, not enough stage time
+
+## 23:30 Checkpoint (1:15)
+
+>> advance: Checkpoint/Restore and Migration
+- A pod restarts, a process moves
+- Many workloads keep state in memory: cache, session, queue consumer, model in flight
+- A zero-maintenance fleet drains a node for an update without killing them
+- I've worked on live migration and checkpoint restore for years on x86 and ARM, at Loophole Labs and now LiveKit
+- Useful for compliance too: a signed quote says the workload runs on a machine in a given region
+- RVA23 profiles make a process portable across implementations
+- RISC-V doing this in 2026 is a proof of maturity
+
+>> advance: What we attest, and when
+- Before: source guest proves its boot chain, quote, nonce, PCR 11, counter
+- After: destination proves the same
+- The checkpoint is the payload, the attestation names the machine that produced it and the machine that runs it
+- Same PID, same instance, same value, before and after
+
+## 24:45 Demo 7 (1:00)
+
+>> advance: Demo 7
+- Demo seven is staged before the talk, about fifteen seconds on stage
+- CRIU freezes the process, warm step uploaded the checkpoint and attested the source
+- The destination downloads, restores, attests after
+- Same PID, same instance, same value
+- The view shows attestation before, upload, download, attestation after
+- Both ends attested, the receiver knows which identity moved the process
+
+## 25:45 Close (1:30)
+
+>> advance: The whole stack, again
+- Last demo, so let me put the whole stack back up
+- We own the silicon: RVA23
+- We own enablement: the finger, the harness, the server
+- We own the firmware: BROM, FSBL, ESOS, OpenSBI, EDK2, systemd-boot, signed UKI
+- We own the image: signed, read-only, updated with sysupdate
+- We own the trust: our TPM, our AK, our update key, our signing key
+- We own the cluster: k3s from a signed extension, and a live process that moved
+- One file for the desktop, one update to version 3, one receipt, one key for consent
+- IoT fleet, phone, tablet, laptop, server, Kubernetes node
+- Not a proprietary service: your keys, your stack
+
+>> advance: The Future
+- RISC-V is the first architecture where every layer can be open at once
+- Profiles make software portable across implementations
+- Hardware that is just as open as software today
+
+>> advance: Thank you
+- Repo, notes, runbooks, slides on the QR code
+- Careers, LiveKit booth 254
+- Feedback QR at the bottom, and I know I talked fast
+- Happy to chat after, and at the booth

